From 4520e21f95e78d6476da8834c804249a7a45eb45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 18:08:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 55/71] inline-3d(#73): express a clipped tile's crop on the
 destination, not the source

A tile clipped at the top of the viewport smeared a "tail" below the
element (and above it when clipped at the bottom): the crop's off-screen
extent came back as a single boundary row replicated for exactly
(element_h - rect_h) rows.

Root cause, measured on hardware with single-frame dumps of every pipeline
stage (weave input / SBS atlas / woven output runtime-side; backdrop /
scratch / isolated canvas resource browser-side, plus an unthrottled
same-frame values line): when the element is viewport-clipped, the WebGL
canvas resource the weave samples is backed by a texture whose PHYSICAL
height is the clipped (visible) height while its logical SkImage size
still claims the full element height. The decisive measurement: the
replicated block starts at scratch row 422 where the
physical-height-equals-rect-height hypothesis predicts 424 (rect 612,
clip 188), reproduced at a second geometry. The source sub-region
therefore walked off the texture and D3D edge-clamp filled the remainder
with the boundary row.

2D-canvas resources (the SDK's image/video windows) are full-size --
verified through the same harness on a matched, clipped video (correctly
scaled fractional src, zero replicated rows) -- which is why only
three.js content ever showed the tail.

Fix: never sub-region the source. Draw the whole image (through the
quad's UV window) at the tile's unclipped position relative to the
clipped scratch and let Skia clip to the surface. The mapping is
identical -- 1:1 vertical, SBS squeeze horizontal, offset by the clip --
but the sampler never leaves the image, so the fix is immune to the
physical-height truncation regardless of the resource's backing. An
unclipped tile reduces to the previous behaviour exactly. The full-image
draw is the one operation that was clean in every capture, on the same
frames where the source crop tailed.

Also records each root resource quad's UVs (TextureDrawQuad
GetNormalizedTexCoords against the provider's backed size) and
visible_rect through SurfaceAggregator -> AggregatedFrame ->
SkiaOutputSurface, so the weave maps through the quad's real UV window
instead of assuming (0,0)-(1,1) -- validated against the video window's
0.5x-scale resource.

Hardware-verified on Leia: tail gone across the clip band, registration
correct, top- and bottom-clip, pics/videos unaffected.
